---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import RatesSim from '../../simulators/rates/RatesSim.astro';
import { ratesQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/rates';

const toc = [
  { id: 'rate', label: 'What is a reaction rate?' },
  { id: 'collision', label: 'Collision theory' },
  { id: 'factors', label: 'Four factors that change the rate' },
  { id: 'graph', label: 'Reading the rate graph' },
  { id: 'formulas', label: 'Formulas' },
  { id: 'terms', label: 'Key terms' },
  { id: 'applications', label: 'Real-world applications' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'model-limits', label: 'What the model simplifies' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Flowers, P., et al. (2019). Chemistry 2e, Section 12.2: Factors Affecting Reaction Rates. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/12-2-factors-affecting-reaction-rates' },
  { text: 'Flowers, P., et al. (2019). Chemistry 2e, Section 12.5: Collision Theory. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/12-5-collision-theory' },
  { text: 'Flowers, P., et al. (2019). Chemistry 2e, Section 12.7: Catalysis. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/12-7-catalysis' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="chemistry"
  topic="reaction-rates"
  description="Interactive reaction rate simulator based on collision theory: change temperature, concentration, surface area and add a catalyst, and measure how fast product forms."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={ratesQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      Some reactions are over in a flash, like fireworks; others take years, like rusting. Chemists can speed reactions
      up or slow them down by controlling just four things. Watch individual particles collide, see which collisions
      succeed, and discover why.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <RatesSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li>Press <strong>Start</strong>. Blue acid particles move around the beaker; the grey solid sits at the bottom. Lighter grey atoms are on the surface and can react.</li>
      <li>Watch for <strong>yellow flashes</strong>: successful collisions that turn an acid particle green (product).</li>
      <li>Change one factor at a time — <strong>temperature</strong>, <strong>concentration</strong>, <strong>surface area</strong> or a <strong>catalyst</strong> — and compare the results.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Save run &amp; restart</strong> to keep a curve on the graph for comparison.</li>
      <li>Click a particle to see whether it has enough energy to react. <strong>Reset</strong> restores the defaults.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="rate">What is a reaction rate?</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>rate of reaction</strong> is how quickly reactants are used up or products are formed. It can be measured
    as the amount of product formed per second, the volume of gas given off per minute, or the time taken for a colour
    change or for a solid to disappear.
  </p>

  <h2 id="collision">Collision theory</h2>
  <p>
    Particles must <strong>collide</strong> to react. But most collisions just bounce off: to react, particles must hit
    with at least a minimum amount of energy, called the <strong>activation energy</strong> (Eₐ), and in a suitable
    orientation. The activation energy is like a hill the particles must climb before the reaction can “roll” on to
    products. Anything that makes successful collisions more frequent increases the rate.
  </p>

  <h2 id="factors">Four factors that change the rate</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Effect</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Temperature</strong> ↑</td><td>Rate ↑ (often strongly)</td><td>Particles move faster, collide more often, and a much larger fraction has energy ≥ Eₐ</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Concentration</strong> (or gas pressure) ↑</td><td>Rate ↑</td><td>More particles in the same space collide more often</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Surface area</strong> ↑</td><td>Rate ↑</td><td>More particles of a solid are exposed and available for collisions</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Catalyst</strong></td><td>Rate ↑</td><td>Provides a pathway with a lower Eₐ, so more collisions succeed</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="graph">Reading the rate graph</h2>
  <p>
    The graph shows the amount of product against time. Its <strong>slope</strong> is the rate: steepest at the start,
    when reactants are plentiful, then flattening as they are used up. A steeper curve means a faster reaction. If the
    same amount of reactant is used, every curve eventually reaches the same final amount of product — the factors change
    <em>how fast</em> the reaction goes, not how much product it can make.
  </p>

  <h2 id="formulas">Formulas</h2>
  <code class="formula">Rate = change in amount of product ÷ time taken</code>
  <p>The fraction of collisions with enough energy depends strongly on temperature (the Arrhenius relationship):</p>
  <code class="formula">k = A · e^(−Eₐ / RT)</code>
  <p>
    Here k is the rate constant, A depends on collision frequency and orientation, R is the gas constant and T is the
    absolute temperature. Lowering Eₐ (a catalyst) or raising T both increase k.
  </p>

  <h2 id="terms">Key terms</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Activation energy (Eₐ)</dt>
    <dd>The minimum energy colliding particles need to react.</dd>
    <dt>Catalyst</dt>
    <dd>A substance that speeds up a reaction without being used up. Biological catalysts are called enzymes.</dd>
    <dt>Successful collision</dt>
    <dd>A collision with enough energy and the right orientation to produce a reaction.</dd>
    <dt>Surface area to volume ratio</dt>
    <dd>How much surface a solid has relative to its size; it increases as a solid is broken into smaller pieces.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="applications">Real-world applications</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Food storage:</strong> refrigerators slow the reactions that spoil food.</li>
    <li><strong>Catalytic converters</strong> in cars use platinum-group metals to convert toxic gases quickly.</li>
    <li><strong>Industry:</strong> the Haber process uses an iron catalyst, high pressure and a carefully chosen temperature to make ammonia fast enough to be economical.</li>
    <li><strong>Safety:</strong> fine dust (flour, coal, sawdust) has so much surface area that it can explode — a serious hazard in mills and mines.</li>
    <li><strong>Your body:</strong> enzymes speed up digestion and metabolism enormously at body temperature.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“Every collision causes a reaction.”</strong> Only a small fraction succeed.</li>
    <li><strong>“A catalyst gives more product.”</strong> It only gives the same product faster.</li>
    <li><strong>“Temperature only makes particles collide more often.”</strong> The main effect is more collisions with enough energy.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="model-limits">What the model simplifies</h2>
  <p>
    The simulator is two-dimensional, ignores orientation, and treats the solid as a grid of identical atoms. Particle
    speeds are randomly refreshed to mimic collisions with water molecules, keeping a temperature-dependent spread of
    energies.
  </p>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Reactions need collisions with energy at least equal to the activation energy.</li>
    <li>Temperature, concentration, surface area and catalysts all increase the rate.</li>
    <li>The slope of a product–time graph is the rate.</li>
    <li>Catalysts lower the activation energy and are not used up.</li>
  </ul>
</SimulatorLayout>
